    vivaQuestions: [
      { question: 'What is the principle of coincidence detection in PET?', answer: 'Coincidence detection identifies pairs of 511 keV gamma photons arriving at opposing detectors within a timing window (4-12 ns). Since the two photons from positron annihilation travel at 180° apart, detecting both localizes the event along the line connecting the detectors, without physical collimators.' },
      { question: 'What is F-18 FDG and why is it the most common PET tracer?', answer: 'F-18 Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) is a glucose analog labeled with fluorine-18. It is taken up by cells like glucose but cannot be metabolized, becoming trapped in cells. Since tumors and inflamed tissues have high glucose metabolism, FDG accumulates in them, making it ideal for oncology imaging.' },
      { question: 'What is the Standardized Uptake Value (SUV) and how is it calculated?', answer: 'SUV is a quantitative measure of radiotracer uptake: SUV = tissue activity concentration (Bq/mL) / (injected dose (Bq) / patient weight (g)). It normalizes uptake for body size and injected dose, enabling comparison between patients and studies. SUVmax is commonly used for tumor characterization.' },
      { question: 'Why are PET and CT combined in hybrid PET-CT scanners?', answer: 'PET-CT combines functional (PET) and anatomical (CT) imaging in one session. CT provides anatomical reference for localizing PET findings, enables attenuation correction of PET data (improving quantification), and adds diagnostic CT information, all in a single patient visit with co-registered images.' },
      { question: 'What is the significance of the 511 keV photon energy in PET?', answer: 'When a positron annihilates with an electron, their mass-energy converts to two 511 keV gamma photons (E = mc², electron rest mass = 511 keV). This specific energy is characteristic of annihilation and allows PET detectors to discriminate true events from background radiation using energy windows.' },
    ],
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'What is the half-life of Fluorine-18 (F-18)?', options: ['20 minutes', '110 minutes', '6 hours', '24 hours'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'F-18 has a half-life of approximately 110 minutes (1.83 hours), requiring rapid transport from the cyclotron to the imaging center and efficient patient scheduling.' },
      { question: 'What energy gamma photons are detected in PET?', options: ['140 keV', '511 keV', '1.25 MeV', '2 MeV'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Positron-electron annihilation produces two 511 keV gamma photons, corresponding to the rest mass energy of an electron (511 keV/c²), detected in coincidence by opposing detectors.' },
      { question: 'What is the typical spatial resolution of a clinical PET scanner?', options: ['1-2 mm', '4-6 mm', '10-15 mm', '20-30 mm'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Clinical PET scanners have a spatial resolution of approximately 4-6 mm, limited by positron range before annihilation, non-colinearity of photons, and detector size.' },
      { question: 'Which radiotracer is most commonly used for oncology PET imaging?', options: ['C-11 methionine', 'F-18 FDG', 'N-13 ammonia', 'O-15 water'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'F-18 FDG (fluorodeoxyglucose) is the most common PET tracer for oncology because tumors have elevated glucose metabolism, causing FDG to accumulate in malignant cells.' },
      { question: 'Why does PET not require physical collimators like SPECT?', options: ['PET uses lower energy photons', 'Electronic collimation via coincidence detection', 'PET has higher resolution inherently', 'PET uses magnetic fields'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'PET uses electronic collimation: coincidence detection of paired photons inherently defines the line of response, eliminating the need for physical collimators that reduce sensitivity in SPECT.' },
    ],
